        "Jacques F. A. P. Miller",
        "Max D. Cooper"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The identification of distinct B- and T-cell lineages established a central organizing principle of adaptive immunity and transformed experimental and clinical immunology.",
        "attribution": "Miller and Cooper are highly appropriate representatives of the complementary experimental discoveries, though the broader development of lymphocyte biology involved many contributors.",
        "maturity": "The conceptual framework and its consequences for immunodeficiency, autoimmunity, transplantation, and immunotherapy have been firmly established over decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery developed through several linked experimental traditions, making the boundary of credit necessarily selective."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B037",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Kazutoshi Mori",
        "Peter Walter"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The unfolded protein response revealed a fundamental system by which cells sense and adapt to endoplasmic-reticulum stress, with broad implications for physiology and disease.",
        "attribution": "Mori and Walter made distinct, foundational contributions to defining conserved UPR signaling mechanisms and are a coherent pair.",
        "maturity": "The pathway is mechanistically mature and its importance across secretory-cell biology, proteostasis, cancer, and degenerative disease is extensively supported.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The clinical translation of UPR manipulation remains less settled than the basic discovery."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B020",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Francis S. Collins",
        "John R. Riordan",
        "Lap-Chee Tsui"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Identification of CFTR provided a decisive molecular explanation for cystic fibrosis and enabled a major era of disease-specific biology and treatment.",
        "attribution": "Collins, Riordan, and Tsui represent the linked mapping, cloning, and functional identification work, although the discovery required large collaborative efforts.",
        "maturity": "The causal role of CFTR and the downstream medical consequences are exceptionally well confirmed, including the development of functional rescue therapies.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The allocation of credit within a large multi-group gene-discovery effort is inherently difficult."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B012",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Ronald M. Evans"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The nuclear receptor superfamily provided a unifying molecular account of ligand-dependent gene control that has reshaped endocrinology, development, and pharmacology.",
        "attribution": "Evans was a leading contributor to the molecular definition and biological interpretation of nuclear hormone receptors, while the field contains other major pioneers.",
        "maturity": "The receptor framework, its physiological roles, and therapeutic relevance have had durable and extensive confirmation.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "A single-name proposal may not capture the full foundational discovery of the receptor superfamily."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B014",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Jens Juul Holst",
        "Svetlana Mojsov",
        "Daniel J. Drucker"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "GLP-1 physiology and its therapeutic exploitation have produced a profound advance in treatment of diabetes and obesity while illuminating endocrine control of metabolism.",
        "attribution": "Holst, Mojsov, and Drucker represent complementary contributions to identifying, characterizing, and establishing the physiological and therapeutic significance of GLP-1.",
        "maturity": "The biological mechanism and extraordinary clinical impact of incretin-based therapies are now well established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The transition from basic hormone biology to optimized therapeutic agents involved substantial additional industrial and clinical contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B047",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Marc Feldmann",
        "Ravinder N. Maini"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "TNF blockade changed the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis and multiple inflammatory disorders, demonstrating that targeted interruption of a cytokine pathway could alter chronic immune-mediated disease.",
        "attribution": "Feldmann and Maini form a clear scientific and clinical partnership linking the TNF hypothesis to successful therapeutic testing.",
        "maturity": "Its therapeutic effect, mechanistic basis, and broad influence on inflammatory medicine have been confirmed over many years.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The work rests on a wider body of cytokine biology and subsequent development by multiple groups and companies."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B029",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Douglas R. Lowy",
        "Ian H. Frazer",
        "John T. Schiller"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Virus-like particle vaccines against HPV created a highly effective means to prevent infection-associated cancers, an outstanding achievement in preventive medicine.",
        "attribution": "Lowy, Frazer, and Schiller are strongly associated with the central VLP vaccine concept and its development, though translation was broadly collaborative.",
        "maturity": "Efficacy against HPV infection and precancerous disease has been repeatedly demonstrated, with population-level cancer-prevention evidence continuing to mature.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The full magnitude of long-term cancer prevention varies with vaccine access, coverage, and follow-up duration."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B021",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Carl H. June",
        "Michel Sadelain",
        "Steven A. Rosenberg"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "CAR-T cell therapy established a powerful new therapeutic modality in which engineered immune cells can induce durable responses in otherwise refractory cancers.",
        "attribution": "June, Sadelain, and Rosenberg made important, complementary contributions to adoptive cell therapy and genetically engineered T-cell approaches.",
        "maturity": "Clinical benefit in several hematologic malignancies is well established and has driven a major expansion of cellular immunotherapy.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Durability, toxicity, access, and effectiveness in solid tumors remain substantial limitations.",
          "The lineage of CAR design and cellular immunotherapy involves numerous essential contributors."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
